// THE EXECUTING CONSUMER OF THE R2 MINT, AND THE ORDER OF ITS CHECKS IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.
// extdeps.cloudflare.account_api_tokens owns the token SHAPE, extdeps.cloudflare.r2 owns the bucket
// resource and the S3 pair derivation, gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint owns the mint POLICY, and
// gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin owns which account and which bucket. None of them could execute,
// because account_id and bucket_name were parameters supplied by nobody. This entry supplies them
// from the declarations and performs the one operation that turns the cluster into a capability.
//
// EVERY PRECONDITION IS DECIDED BEFORE THE CREATE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION. AccountTokens.Create
// is non-idempotent and its value is returned once and never again: a Create issued before the
// custody container is known to be reachable mints a credential this run can then fail to store, and
// the token exists on the account with nobody holding it. So the sequence is decide, fetch, create,
// store -- and the two facts this fleet does not yet have (an allocated account id, a pinned
// bootstrap custody version) refuse HERE, typed and located, with nothing spent.
//
// THE ABSENT-CREDENTIAL REFUSAL IS THE DISCRIMINATING RED, AND IT IS ON THE REAL PATH TODAY.
// gunbc#10739 named the defect in advance: a fabrication placed downstream of the check that catches
// the credential's absence -- fetch nothing, then write an empty pair. Here the absence is an ARM of
// gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin's BootstrapCustodyStanding rather than an empty value flowing on, so
// there is no downstream position in which a fabricated pair could be constructed. classify_r2_mint
// is pure and total, so both the refusal and its accepted positive control are executable evidence.
//
// THE MUTATION HAS NOW RUN. gunbc#10929 made Map-valued effect inputs recordable. This entry
// invoked cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create, stored the token VALUE (not the derived S3 secret)
// through gunbc.secret_provision, and pinned the origin read credential to the verified version.
// Nested Create projections use JSON-pointer slashes (result/id), matching AccessVersion's
// payload/data, because a dotted path is a single JSON-pointer key and was why the first live
// Create looked like RestOk with a null status.
//
// THE ATTEMPT GRAIN WAS THE THIRD CREATE. THREE Creates ran. The first two reached
// Cloudflare under the dotted-path decoder, minted live tokens whose values this process did not
// keep, and were revoked by id after List: 3cbb99f150c4daec03ab6a8458a2440f and
// cb444492f55dbc1b40ee7ce17778d7ff (account listing was the only readback of those two). The stored
// mint is 3f43e1c7f8fbcfc97139cb5d9dc4e984. Those identities are named here because the PR body must
// not be the only record of what the account saw; they are not re-derived by comparing them to
// themselves in a witness. The instrument is gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint_run run / List /
// revoke_unheld_object_read_token. Those were not commit-ambiguous HTTP arms; they were RestOk with
// unread fields. A retry into a 5xx/timeout was not done. The class is
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.json_pointer_dot_spelling_reads_nested_fields_as_absent.
//
// THE ACCESS KEY ID IS THE TOKEN ID AND IS NOT A SECRET. The token value is not in this file.

// THE ADMITTED REQUEST IS CARRIED WITH THE ACCOUNT AND THE LOCUS IT WAS ADMITTED FOR, never beside
// them. A request admitted for account A paired at the call site with account B's id would address
// the wrong account's token endpoint while carrying A's bucket resource in its policy, and every
// field would individually be true -- the argument-position confusion OwnedCredentialSnapshot exists
// to prevent one layer down, arriving on the mint path.
type R2MintPreconditions
  = R2MintReady {
      account_id: CloudflareAccountId
      bootstrap_locus: SecretRef
      request: CreateAccountTokenRequest
    }
  | R2MintBootstrapCustodyUnrecorded { intervention: HumanIntervention }
  | R2MintCustodyContainerAbsent { intervention: HumanIntervention }
  | R2MintAccountUnallocated { intervention: HumanIntervention }
  | R2MintOriginUnallocated { purpose: BucketPurpose }
  | R2MintAdmissionRefused { standing: AccountTokenApiMintStanding }

// CUSTODY IS CHECKED FIRST, BEFORE THE ACCOUNT, because the credential is the precondition without
// which nothing downstream can happen and because it needs no other fact to decide. Checking the
// account first would report the account gap to an operator whose next obstacle is the credential
// anyway, and would leave the credential-absent arm unreachable on the real path -- an arm that
// cannot be reached is an arm nobody can trust.
// WHICH TOKEN THIS RUN MINTS, AS A PARAMETER OF ONE FOLD RATHER THAN A SECOND FOLD. The read and
// write mints differ in three facts -- the grant, the token name, and the custody container the
// value is stored into -- and in nothing else: the same account, the same bootstrap, the same
// bucket, the same Create, the same store bracket. Copying classify_r2_mint for the write profile
// would have put the five-arm precondition ladder in two places, drifting on the first arm either
// copy learned about. The profile is decided at the entry (run / run_object_write), which is the
// dispatch-is-realization seam DESIGN section 3 names.
type R2OriginTokenProfile
  = R2OriginObjectRead
  | R2OriginObjectWrite

// THE PROFILE'S NAME SUFFIX IS ITS OWN FACT, because two things need it and only one of them has a
// bucket: the token NAME is bucket + suffix, and the receipt GLOB is prefix + wildcard + suffix,
// built where no bucket is in scope. Spelling "-object-write" in both places is the §3 fork that
// makes a rename silent in exactly one of them.
fn r2_origin_token_name_suffix(profile: R2OriginTokenProfile) -> String {
  match profile {
    R2OriginObjectRead => "-object-read"
    R2OriginObjectWrite => "-object-write"
  }
}

fn r2_origin_token_name(profile: R2OriginTokenProfile, bucket: R2OriginBucket) -> String {
  join([bucket.bucket_name as String, r2_origin_token_name_suffix(profile: profile)], "")
}

fn r2_origin_token_secret_id(profile: R2OriginTokenProfile) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match profile {
    R2OriginObjectRead => fabric_durable_origin_read_secret_id
    R2OriginObjectWrite => fabric_durable_origin_write_secret_id
  }
}

fn r2_origin_token_credential(profile: R2OriginTokenProfile, bucket: R2OriginBucket) -> OriginCredentialStanding {
  match profile {
    R2OriginObjectRead => bucket.read_credential
    R2OriginObjectWrite => bucket.write_credential
  }
}

fn admit_r2_origin_token_mint(
  profile: R2OriginTokenProfile,
  name: String,
  account_id: String,
  jurisdiction: R2Jurisdiction,
  bucket_name: String,
) -> AccountTokenApiMintStanding {
  match profile {
    R2OriginObjectRead =>
      admit_r2_object_read_api_mint(
        name: name, account_id: account_id, jurisdiction: jurisdiction, bucket_name: bucket_name)
    R2OriginObjectWrite =>
      admit_r2_object_write_api_mint(
        name: name, account_id: account_id, jurisdiction: jurisdiction, bucket_name: bucket_name)
  }
}

// THE CONTAINER STANDING CARRIES THE OBSERVATION SEAL RATHER THAN A Bool. A boolean "the container
// exists" would be re-derived into a write authorization somewhere downstream, which is exactly the
// seam gunbc.secret_provision_actuator's sole_constructor seals exist to close: the authorization to
// add a version is derived only from a GET whose returned resource actually named the requested
// project and secret.
type R2MintCustodyContainerStanding
  = R2MintCustodyContainerPresent { container: ObservedSecretContainer }
  | R2MintCustodyContainerNotObserved { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | R2MintCustodyContainerUnobserved { intervention: HumanIntervention }

// THE PURE FOLD TAKES THE VERDICT, NOT THE SEAL, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS IT WITNESSABLE.
// ObservedSecretContainer is sole_constructor, so no module but gunbc.secret_provision_actuator can
// build one -- correct, and it means any fold downstream of it that takes the seal directly cannot be
// exercised by a witness at all. classify_r2_mint is the pure admission ladder and its job is to
// DECIDE, so it needs only "was the container admitted, and if not what does the operator do". The
// seal never enters it; r2_mint_bootstrap already holds it and carries it straight to the store.
// THE THIRD ARM IS NOT A FAIL-OPEN, IT IS WHICH OPERATION IS ASKING. The custody container is a
// precondition OF THE WRITE, and r2_mint_bootstrap is shared by four entries of which only two
// write: List and revoke read the account's tokens through the Cloudflare bootstrap credential and
// touch no Secret Manager container at all. An earlier revision observed the container inside the
// shared bracket, which gated RECOVERY on the very resource whose absence recovery exists to clean
// up after -- the orphan receipt tells an operator to run list_account_tokens_for_reconcile to find
// and revoke the live token, and that command would have inherited a GetSecret requirement on a
// secret the mint's own grant cells do not even cover for the read profile. A remedy that depends
// on the thing that failed is not a remedy.
//
// So the admission is supplied BY THE ENTRY rather than observed by the bracket. ContainerNotRequired
// carries its reason so the arm cannot be reached for an operation that does write: it is a claim
// about the caller's effects, and a writing caller that passed it would be making a false one.
type R2MintContainerAdmission
  = ContainerAdmitted
  | ContainerNotRequired { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | ContainerAbsent { intervention: HumanIntervention }

// WHAT THE ENTRY ASKS THE BRACKET TO DO ABOUT THE CONTAINER. This is the caller's statement of its
// own effects, not a switch: an entry that will add a Secret Manager version asks for the
// observation, and an entry that only reads the account's tokens says so and carries the reason.
type R2MintContainerRequirement
  = ObserveCustodyContainer
  | CustodyContainerNotNeeded { reason: NonEmptyStr }

data r2_mint_container_not_required_reason: NonEmptyStr = "this entry reads the account's tokens through the Cloudflare bootstrap credential and writes no Secret Manager version, so the custody container is not its precondition" as NonEmptyStr

fn r2_mint_container_admission(standing: R2MintCustodyContainerStanding) -> R2MintContainerAdmission {
  match standing {
    R2MintCustodyContainerPresent { container: _ } => ContainerAdmitted
    R2MintCustodyContainerNotObserved { reason: reason } => ContainerNotRequired { reason: reason }
    R2MintCustodyContainerUnobserved { intervention: intervention } =>
      ContainerAbsent { intervention: intervention }
  }
}

data r2_mint_custody_container_intervention_identity: String = "cloudflare-r2-origin-write-custody-container"

// THIS BURDEN HAS A REAL DISCHARGE SITE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT NoDischargeSiteModeled. The two
// interventions beside it in gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin are dashboard ceremonies nothing in this
// tree can observe. This one is discharged by the very fold that raises it: the container GET is the
// discharge check, so "has the operator done it" is answered by execution rather than by a human
// remembering.
fn r2_mint_custody_container_intervention(secret_id: NonEmptyStr, cause: NonEmptyStr) -> HumanIntervention {
  HumanIntervention {
    identity: r2_mint_custody_container_intervention_identity as HumanInterventionIdentity,
    frequency: FleetOnce,
    subject: join(["the Secret Manager custody container ", secret_id as String], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    instruction: join([
      "THE CUSTODY CONTAINER COULD NOT BE OBSERVED: ", cause as String,
      " -- create the empty secret ", secret_id as String,
      " in ", fleet_secrets_gcp_project as String,
      " from an operator gcloud session and converge the cells declared in gunbc.cloudflare.r2_mint_secret_access, then dispatch again. NOTHING WAS CREATED AT CLOUDFLARE.",
    ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    completion_evidence: DischargedAt {
      evidence: decl_ref(
        module_path: "gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint_run",
        decl_name: "observe_r2_mint_custody_container",
      ),
    },
  }
}

fn observe_r2_mint_custody_container(
  profile: R2OriginTokenProfile,
  gcp_token: Secret,
) -> R2MintCustodyContainerStanding uses net: Network {
  let secret_id = r2_origin_token_secret_id(profile: profile)
  match observe_secret_container(
    project: fleet_secret_ref(secret_id: secret_id).project,
    secret_id: secret_id,
    token: gcp_token,
  ) {
    SecretObservationRefused { cause: cause } =>
      R2MintCustodyContainerUnobserved {
        intervention: r2_mint_custody_container_intervention(secret_id: secret_id, cause: cause),
      }
    SecretContainerAbsent { project: _, secret_id: absent_id } =>
      R2MintCustodyContainerUnobserved {
        intervention: r2_mint_custody_container_intervention(secret_id: secret_id, cause: concat("no custody container named ", concat(absent_id as String, " exists in the project; this run does not create it")) as NonEmptyStr),
      }
    SecretContainerObserved { container: container } =>
      R2MintCustodyContainerPresent { container: container }
  }
}

// THE DESTINATION CONTAINER IS A PRECONDITION, DECIDED BEFORE THE CREATE, AND THAT ORDERING IS THE
// WHOLE OF THIS ARM. This module's own annotation has always claimed "every precondition is decided
// before the Create", and for the custody CONTAINER it was not true: store_minted_token called
// CreateSecret AFTER cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create had already minted a token whose value is
// returned exactly once. #10947 succeeded only because the operator's identity happened to hold
// project-wide secretmanager.secrets.create; a federated least-privilege identity does not, and the
// failure that would have followed is not a failed run but a live credential on the account that
// nobody holds.
//
// THE REMEDY IS NOT TO GRANT create, IT IS TO STOP NEEDING IT. secretmanager.secrets.create is a
// PROJECT-level permission and cannot be a gunbc.auth.gcp_secret_access cell (one secret, one role),
// and no per-secret cell can be written for a secret that does not exist. So the container is
// established by the operator ceremony that grants the cells, this fold refuses when it is absent,
// and the write path is AddSecretVersion only. Mapping a 409 AFTER the Create to "container present"
// would have left the irreversible effect ahead of the check, which is the defect rather than a fix
// for it (operator ruling, 2026-09-12).
// ADMITTED AND NOT-REQUIRED PROCEED IDENTICALLY, SO THE LADDER IS WRITTEN ONCE. Listing both arms
// against a copy of the whole ladder would be the same fold twice, and the two would drift.
// THE SEAL TRAVELS AS AN OPTIONAL BECAUSE ONLY SOME ENTRIES HAVE ONE, and the writer is where its
// absence refuses. List and revoke legitimately carry none: they were never going to add a version.
// The mint path asks for the observation and therefore refuses on none at the point it would write.
fn observed_container_or_none(standing: R2MintCustodyContainerStanding) -> ObservedSecretContainer? {
  match standing {
    R2MintCustodyContainerPresent { container: container } => Present { value: container }
    R2MintCustodyContainerNotObserved { reason: _ } => none
    R2MintCustodyContainerUnobserved { intervention: _ } => none
  }
}

fn container_admission_refusal(container: R2MintContainerAdmission) -> HumanIntervention? {
  match container {
    ContainerAbsent { intervention: intervention } => Present { value: intervention }
    ContainerAdmitted => none
    ContainerNotRequired { reason: _ } => none
  }
}

fn classify_r2_mint(
  profile: R2OriginTokenProfile,
  account: FleetCloudflareAccountStanding,
  custody: BootstrapCustodyStanding,
  origin: R2OriginStanding,
  container: R2MintContainerAdmission,
) -> R2MintPreconditions {
  match container_admission_refusal(container: container) {
    Present { value: container_intervention } =>
      R2MintCustodyContainerAbsent { intervention: container_intervention }
    Absent =>
  match custody {
    BootstrapCustodyUnrecorded { intervention: intervention } =>
      R2MintBootstrapCustodyUnrecorded { intervention: intervention }
    BootstrapCustodyPinned { locus: locus } =>
      match origin {
        R2OriginUnallocated { purpose: purpose } =>
          R2MintOriginUnallocated { purpose: purpose }
        R2OriginAllocated { bucket: bucket } =>
      match account {
        CloudflareAccountUnallocated { company: _, intervention: intervention } =>
          R2MintAccountUnallocated { intervention: intervention }
        CloudflareAccountAllocated { company: _, account_id: account_id } =>
          match admit_r2_origin_token_mint(
            profile: profile,
            name: r2_origin_token_name(profile: profile, bucket: bucket),
            account_id: account_id as String,
            jurisdiction: bucket.jurisdiction,
            bucket_name: bucket.bucket_name as String,
          ) {
            AccountTokenApiMintAdmitted { shape: _, request: request } =>
              R2MintReady {
                account_id: account_id,
                bootstrap_locus: locus,
                request: request,
              }
            AccountTokenApiMintRefusedHumanDashboardOnly { shape: s } =>
              R2MintAdmissionRefused { standing: AccountTokenApiMintRefusedHumanDashboardOnly { shape: s } }
            AccountTokenApiMintRefusedGrantPolicyMismatch { shape: s, request: r } =>
              R2MintAdmissionRefused { standing: AccountTokenApiMintRefusedGrantPolicyMismatch { shape: s, request: r } }
            AccountTokenApiMintRefusedLifetimeMismatch { shape: s, request: r } =>
              R2MintAdmissionRefused { standing: AccountTokenApiMintRefusedLifetimeMismatch { shape: s, request: r } }
          }
      }
      }
  }
  }
}

// THE BURDEN IS RENDERED FROM THE PRICED INTERVENTIONS RATHER THAN RESTATED, which is what makes
// cloudflare_r2_token_human_burden consumed by execution rather than merely declared. A refusal that
// spelled the dashboard ceremony in its own words would be a second copy of an instruction the
// burden already owns, and the two would drift.
fn burden_instructions(burden: ProvisioningHumanBurden) -> String {
  join(map(burden.interventions, i => i.instruction as String), " | ")
}

fn bootstrap_burden_text() -> String {
  burden_instructions(burden: cloudflare_r2_token_human_burden())
}

// ONE EFFECTFUL BRACKET FOR THE FOUR ENTRIES. classify_r2_mint is the pure admission fold; this
// is the custody fetch that every Create/List/Delete/AccessVersion consumer has to pass through.
// Re-authoring the five classify arms and the five fetch arms at each entry was four copies of one
// refusal text, which is how a decode-arm drift becomes a second mint.
// THE IDENTITY THIS RUN HAS, RESOLVED ONCE AND USED FOR EVERY EFFECT IT PERFORMS. The mint reads
// the bootstrap credential AND then writes the custody container and its version, so one token has
// to survive from the read to the writes. Before this it was an operator token by construction --
// read_supplied_access_token() then OperatorSuppliedToken, spelled here -- which is why the only
// executed precedent (#10947) was an operator pasting a one-hour ya29 token into a session, and why
// no federated workflow run could perform this mint at all.
//
// gunbc.auth.access_token_source resolve_access_token names the arm the RUN's environment supports
// (workload federation in a dispatched job, an operator token file in a session) and realizes it.
// This module chooses nothing about identity, spells no arm, and does not compose the selection
// itself: adding a fourth realizer there reaches this mint without an edit here.

type R2MintBootstrapStanding
  = R2MintBootstrapReady {
      account_id: CloudflareAccountId
      request: CreateAccountTokenRequest
      bootstrap: Secret
      gcp_token: Secret
      resolved_version: SmResolvedVersionIdentity
      container: ObservedSecretContainer?
    }
  | R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE ACCESS TOKEN IS RESOLVED BEFORE THE LADDER RUNS, AND THAT REORDERING IS DELIBERATE. The
// container precondition is an OBSERVATION, so it needs the run's identity before it can be decided,
// and a precondition that cannot be evaluated is not a precondition. The cost is that a run with no
// credential at all now reports that first rather than reporting the account or custody gap; the
// benefit is that the one check whose absence could burn a non-idempotent Cloudflare Create is
// inside the ladder rather than downstream of it. Nothing here reaches the network except the
// container GET, which is readonly.
fn r2_mint_bootstrap(
  profile: R2OriginTokenProfile,
  requirement: R2MintContainerRequirement,
) -> R2MintBootstrapStanding uses net: Network {
  match resolve_access_token() {
    AccessTokenUpsertRequired { plan: plan } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused {
        cause: concat("NO GCP ACCESS TOKEN: ", access_token_upsert_required_reason(plan: plan)) as NonEmptyStr,
      }
    AccessTokenEnsureRefused { reason: reason } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: concat("NO GCP ACCESS TOKEN: ", reason as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    AccessTokenReady { token: gcp_token } => {
      let container_standing = match requirement {
        CustodyContainerNotNeeded { reason: reason } => R2MintCustodyContainerNotObserved { reason: reason }
        ObserveCustodyContainer => observe_r2_mint_custody_container(profile: profile, gcp_token: gcp_token)
      }
      match classify_r2_mint(
    profile: profile,
    account: fleet_cloudflare_account_standing(),
    custody: fleet_cloudflare_bootstrap_custody(),
    origin: fabric_durable_origin_standing(),
    container: r2_mint_container_admission(standing: container_standing),
  ) {
    R2MintCustodyContainerAbsent { intervention: intervention } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: intervention.instruction }
    R2MintBootstrapCustodyUnrecorded { intervention: intervention } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: concat("NO BOOTSTRAP TOKEN IN CUSTODY: ", intervention.instruction as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    R2MintAccountUnallocated { intervention: intervention } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: concat("NO CLOUDFLARE ACCOUNT ID: ", intervention.instruction as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    R2MintOriginUnallocated { purpose: purpose } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: concat("NO BUCKET ALLOCATED FOR ", bucket_purpose_key(purpose: purpose)) as NonEmptyStr }
    R2MintAdmissionRefused { standing: standing } =>
      R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: concat("THE MINT WAS NOT ADMITTED: ", mint_refusal_text(standing: standing)) as NonEmptyStr }
    R2MintReady { account_id: account_id, bootstrap_locus: locus, request: request } =>
      {
        let container = observed_container_or_none(standing: container_standing)
          match fetch_secret_ref_credential_with_token(
            secret_ref: locus,
            access_token: gcp_token,
          ) {
            SecretCredentialAccessUpsertRequired { plan: _ } =>
              R2MintBootstrapRefused {
                cause: "THE BOOTSTRAP TOKEN COULD NOT BE READ: the access ensure requires an interactive authentication that this run cannot perform" as NonEmptyStr,
              }
            SecretCredentialFetchRefused { reason: reason } =>
              R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: concat("THE BOOTSTRAP TOKEN COULD NOT BE READ: ", reason as String) as NonEmptyStr }
            SecretCredentialWireDecodeRefused { identity_cause: _, payload_cause: _ } =>
              R2MintBootstrapRefused {
                cause: "THE BOOTSTRAP TOKEN COULD NOT BE READ: the access response did not decode" as NonEmptyStr,
              }
            SecretCredentialResolvedVersionMismatch { requested: requested, resolved: resolved } =>
              R2MintBootstrapRefused {
                cause: concat(concat(
                  "THE BOOTSTRAP READ ANSWERED ABOUT ANOTHER VERSION: asked for ", requested),
                  concat(", answered about ", resolved)) as NonEmptyStr,
              }
            SecretCredentialReady { credential: bootstrap, resolved_version: identity } =>
              R2MintBootstrapReady {
                account_id: account_id,
                request: request,
                bootstrap: bootstrap,
                gcp_token: gcp_token,
                resolved_version: identity,
                container: container,
              }
          }
      }
  }
      }
  }
}

type R2AccountTokenListing
  = R2AccountTokensListed { tokens: List<AccountTokenListRow> }
  | R2AccountTokenListRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn list_account_tokens(
  bootstrap: Secret,
  account_id: CloudflareAccountId,
) -> R2AccountTokenListing uses net: Network {
  let listed = cloudflare.AccountTokens.List(
    auth_token: bootstrap,
    account_id: account_id as String,
  )
  match listed.outcome {
    RestStatusRefused { status: _, body: body } =>
      R2AccountTokenListRefused { cause: concat("TOKEN LIST REFUSED: ", body) as NonEmptyStr }
    RestTransportRefused { cause: cause } =>
      R2AccountTokenListRefused { cause: concat("TOKEN LIST GOT NO RESPONSE: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    RestBodyUndecodable { status: _, cause: cause } =>
      R2AccountTokenListRefused { cause: concat("TOKEN LIST BODY DID NOT DECODE: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    RestOk =>
      R2AccountTokensListed { tokens: listed.tokens }
  }
}

// THE PIN, NOT A TRANSCRIBED TOKEN ID, IS WHETHER ORIGIN-READ CUSTODY EXISTS. The stored secret
// is the token VALUE; listing does not return it, so there is no live join from Secret Manager
// bytes to a Cloudflare token id. An exact version on the origin read_credential is the live
// declaration that a held mint exists. Comparing a listed id to a receipt literal goes stale on
// the next rotation and deletes the credential the fabric is reading with.
fn origin_credential_pin_is_exact(credential: OriginCredentialStanding) -> Bool {
  match credential {
    OriginCredentialUnminted { mint_entry: _ } => false
    OriginCredentialMintEntryUnbuilt { secret_id: _ } => false
    OriginCredentialPinned { access_key_id: _, token_value: locus } =>
      (locus.version as String) != "latest"
        && (locus.version as String) != "pending"
  }
}

fn origin_read_pin_is_exact(origin: R2OriginStanding) -> Bool {
  match origin {
    R2OriginUnallocated { purpose: _ } => false
    R2OriginAllocated { bucket: bucket } =>
      origin_credential_pin_is_exact(credential: bucket.read_credential)
  }
}

// THE MINT ITSELF, REACHED ONLY WITH THE BOOTSTRAP CREDENTIAL IN HAND.
//
// THE TRANSPORT OUTCOME IS READ RATHER THAN INFERRED. A non-idempotent create whose answer was lost
// may already have minted a token, so "the service refused" and "the outcome is unknown" have
// opposite remedies and neither may be reported as the other. That distinction is why this change
// added outcome: RestOutcome to the operation; without it a 5xx and a refusal were the same silence.
//
// THE 5xx SPLIT IS CONSUMED, NOT RESTATED. gunbc.secret_provision_actuator already owns
// mutation_status_is_commit_ambiguous and states why the boundary sits at 500: a 4xx is a decision
// the remote made BEFORE committing anything, while a 5xx spans states the remote itself may not be
// able to describe, including a write that landed under a handler that then failed. Spelling a
// second threshold here would be one fact with two authorities, and the two would drift on the first
// status either side learned about. An earlier revision of this function DID map every refused
// status to the refused arm -- so a 500 that may have minted a token was classified as safely
// retryable, which is the duplicate-write class arriving through an HTTP response instead of a
// transport loss.
//
// AND IT IS WORSE HERE THAN AT A SECRET VERSION, which is why the reuse is not merely tidy. A
// duplicate AddVersion writes bytes we hold; a duplicate Create mints a CREDENTIAL whose value is
// shown once, so a retry after an ambiguous create can leave a live token on the account that
// nothing in this fleet holds or can revoke by id.
type R2TokenMintOutcome
  = R2TokenMinted { token: CreatedAccountToken, pair: R2S3AccessPair }
  | R2TokenMintRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | R2TokenMintOutcomeUnknown { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn mint_r2_origin_token(
  account_id: CloudflareAccountId,
  request: CreateAccountTokenRequest,
  bootstrap: Secret,
) -> R2TokenMintOutcome uses net: Network {
  let created = cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create(
    auth_token: bootstrap,
    account_id: account_id as String,
    name: request.name,
    policies: request.policies,
    not_before: request.not_before,
    expires_on: request.expires_on,
  )
  match created.outcome {
    RestStatusRefused { status: status, body: body } =>
      if mutation_status_is_commit_ambiguous(status: status) {
        R2TokenMintOutcomeUnknown {
          cause: concat(
            "AccountTokens.Create answered with a server-error status, which does not establish that the mutation did not commit; a token may exist on the account whose value -- returned once -- was never read: ",
            body) as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      } else {
        R2TokenMintRefused {
          cause: concat("Cloudflare refused the token create: ", body) as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      }
    RestTransportRefused { cause: cause } =>
      R2TokenMintOutcomeUnknown {
        cause: concat(
          "no response arrived for AccountTokens.Create, so a token may exist on the account with nobody holding its value; reconcile by listing the account's tokens rather than creating again: ",
          cause as String) as NonEmptyStr,
      }
    RestBodyUndecodable { status: _, cause: cause } =>
      R2TokenMintOutcomeUnknown {
        cause: concat(
          "AccountTokens.Create answered with a body this client could not decode, so the outcome is unread rather than known and the value -- returned once -- may be lost: ",
          cause as String) as NonEmptyStr,
      }
    RestOk =>
      match created_account_token_from_create_response(
        response: CreatedAccountTokenCreateResponse {
          id: created.id,
          name: created.name,
          status: created.status,
          value: created.value,
          expires_on: created.expires_on,
          not_before: created.not_before,
        }
      ) {
        Absent =>
          R2TokenMintOutcomeUnknown {
            cause: concat(
              "AccountTokens.Create reported success with a status this model does not recognize, so a token exists whose state is unknown and whose value this run cannot safely store: ",
              created.status) as NonEmptyStr,
          }
        Present { value: token } =>
          R2TokenMinted {
            token: token,
            pair: r2_s3_access_pair_from_created_token(token: token),
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE WRITE-BACK GOES THROUGH THE SAME BRACKET THE PRINTER CREDENTIAL USES, and the value it stores
// is the TOKEN VALUE rather than the derived S3 pair. extdeps.cloudflare.r2 states the derivation as
// a cited fact -- the access key id IS the created token's id, and the S3 secret IS the SHA-256 of
// the create value -- so storing the value keeps ONE authority for the credential and lets both
// halves of the pair be derived from it. Storing the derived secret beside it would be a second
// representation of the same fact, and a wrong one the moment the derivation is restated.
//
// THE ACCESS KEY ID IS NOT A SECRET AND IS REPORTED, NOT STORED IN CUSTODY. It is the token's id,
// which the account's own token listing shows. The two revoked unread Creates are named only in the
// annotation at the top of this module and in the PR body; the stored mint's id is consumed, so it
// is a pin -- gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin fabric_durable_origin_read_access_key_id -- beside the
// version pin it rotates with, because the S3 signer needs it as the access key
// (extdeps.cloudflare.r2 R2S3AccessPair). A transcribed Secret Manager resource string would rot
// the moment the pin rotated.

// WHAT HAPPENED TO THE VALUE IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS WHETHER THE RUN SUCCEEDED, and collapsing
// them is how a receipt tells an operator something false. Every arm below fails the run; they do
// NOT agree about the bytes. NothingWasWritten is a definite negative -- the snapshot never became a
// version, so the token's value is gone and only a revoke can clean up. WriteOutcomeUnknown is not a
// negative at all: AddVersion may have committed with the answer lost, so a second attempt could
// double-write and "the value is lost" would be a fabricated certainty (DESIGN section 5). A version
// really did land for VersionLandedUnverified, at a known identity, and the correct remedy is to
// DISABLE that version rather than to revoke and forget it.
//
// The first revision of the orphan receipt printed stored=no for all three. That is the same defect
// this PR filed a failure-mode row about, one layer up: a receipt asserting a state the execution
// does not establish.
type R2MintedCustodyStanding
  = NothingWasWritten
  | WriteOutcomeUnknown
  | VersionLandedUnverified { version: NonEmptyStr }

fn minted_custody_line(custody: R2MintedCustodyStanding) -> String {
  match custody {
    NothingWasWritten => "stored=no"
    WriteOutcomeUnknown => "stored=unknown"
    VersionLandedUnverified { version: version } =>
      join(["stored=unverified\nversion=", version as String], "")
  }
}

fn minted_custody_action(custody: R2MintedCustodyStanding) -> String {
  match custody {
    NothingWasWritten =>
      "action=REVOKE THIS TOKEN: it is live on the account, its value was returned once and is lost, and nothing was written."
    WriteOutcomeUnknown =>
      "action=DO NOT RE-RUN THE MINT. The version add may have committed with its answer lost, so a second mint would double-write. List the secret's versions first, then revoke this token only if no version carries it."
    VersionLandedUnverified { version: _ } =>
      "action=DISABLE THE VERSION NAMED ABOVE rather than rewriting it, then decide on the token: a version did land, so this is not the lost-value case."
  }
}

type R2CredentialStored
  = R2CredentialStoredAt { version_resource: String }
  | R2CredentialStoreRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr, custody: R2MintedCustodyStanding }

// THE CONTAINER IS AN ARGUMENT, NOT SOMETHING THIS FUNCTION CREATES. It arrives as the seal
// r2_mint_bootstrap observed BEFORE the Cloudflare Create, so by the time this runs the destination
// is a confirmed fact rather than a write about to be attempted. The CreateSecret that used to sit
// here is gone: it was the one effect in this flow placed after the irreversible one, and the only
// reason #10947 survived it was that the operator's identity held project-wide secrets.create.
fn store_minted_token(
  token: CreatedAccountToken,
  locus: SecretRef,
  gcp_token: Secret,
  container: ObservedSecretContainer,
) -> R2CredentialStored uses net: Network {
  match snapshot_minted_secret(payload: token.value) {
    CredentialSnapshotRefused { cause: cause } =>
      R2CredentialStoreRefused {
        cause: concat(
          "THE TOKEN WAS MINTED AND COULD NOT BE CAPTURED FOR STORAGE, so its value -- returned once -- is lost and the token must be revoked from the account: ",
          cause as String) as NonEmptyStr,
                custody: NothingWasWritten,
              }
    CredentialSnapshotRefusedResidueRemains { workspace: workspace, cause: cause } =>
      R2CredentialStoreRefused {
        cause: concat(concat(
          "THE TOKEN WAS MINTED, NOT STORED, AND PLAINTEXT MAY REMAIN AT ", workspace as String),
          concat(": ", cause as String)) as NonEmptyStr,
                custody: NothingWasWritten,
              }
    CredentialSnapshotTaken { snapshot: snapshot } => {
      let attempt = concat("cloudflare-r2-origin-token-mint:", locus.secret as String) as SecretProvisionAttemptIdentity
      let capability = version_add_capability_for_observed_container(
        container: container,
        attempt: attempt,
        snapshot: snapshot,
      )
      match add_initial_version(capability: capability, token: gcp_token) {
            VersionAdditionRefused { cause: cause } =>
              R2CredentialStoreRefused {
                cause: concat("THE TOKEN WAS MINTED BUT ITS VERSION WAS REFUSED: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr,
                custody: NothingWasWritten,
              }
            VersionAdditionOutcomeUnknown { cause: cause } =>
              R2CredentialStoreRefused {
                cause: concat("THE TOKEN WAS MINTED AND THE VERSION OUTCOME IS UNKNOWN: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr,
                custody: WriteOutcomeUnknown,
              }
            VersionAddedButUnverified { version: version, cause: cause } =>
              R2CredentialStoreRefused {
                cause: concat(concat(concat("A VERSION LANDED AT ", version as String), " BUT DID NOT VERIFY: "),
                  concat(cause as String, " -- disable that version rather than rewriting it")) as NonEmptyStr,
                custody: VersionLandedUnverified { version: version as NonEmptyStr },
              }
            VersionAddedAndVerified { verified: verified } =>
              R2CredentialStoredAt { version_resource: verified_exact_ref(verified) as String }
      }
    }
  }
}

// List and revoke are the standing reconcile entries for a non-idempotent Create whose HTTP
// outcome is unknown or whose nested fields were unread. They are gunbc-run functions, the same
// consumer class as run(); they are not a second mint path. /tmp is the non-secret readback
// channel run() also uses. They dissolve when a modeled artifact store replaces that write.

fn list_account_tokens_for_reconcile() -> ProcessExit uses net: Network {
  match r2_mint_bootstrap(
    profile: R2OriginObjectRead,
    requirement: CustodyContainerNotNeeded { reason: r2_mint_container_not_required_reason },
  ) {
    R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: cause } =>
      exit_failure(reason: cause as String)
    R2MintBootstrapReady {
      account_id: account_id,
      request: _,
      bootstrap: bootstrap,
      gcp_token: _,
      resolved_version: _,
      container: _,
    } =>
      match list_account_tokens(bootstrap: bootstrap, account_id: account_id) {
        R2AccountTokenListRefused { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: cause as String)
        R2AccountTokensListed { tokens: tokens } => {
          let lines = map(tokens, row => join([row.id as String, row.name, row.status], "\t"))
          let written = Filesystem.Write(
            path: "/tmp/r2-account-token-list.txt",
            content: join(lines, "\n"),
          )
          if written.success {
            ExitSuccess
          } else {
            exit_failure(reason: concat("LIST SUCCEEDED AND THE FILE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN: ", written.error))
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn revoke_unheld_object_read_token() -> ProcessExit uses net: Network {
  match r2_mint_bootstrap(
    profile: R2OriginObjectRead,
    requirement: CustodyContainerNotNeeded { reason: r2_mint_container_not_required_reason },
  ) {
    R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: cause } =>
      exit_failure(reason: cause as String)
    R2MintBootstrapReady {
      account_id: account_id,
      request: request,
      bootstrap: bootstrap,
      gcp_token: _,
      resolved_version: _,
      container: _,
    } =>
      match list_account_tokens(bootstrap: bootstrap, account_id: account_id) {
        R2AccountTokenListRefused { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: cause as String)
        R2AccountTokensListed { tokens: tokens } => {
          let hit_count = fold(
            tokens,
            init: 0,
            f: (n, row) => if row.name == request.name { n + 1 } else { n },
          )
          if hit_count == 0 {
            ExitSuccess
          } else {
            if hit_count != 1 {
              exit_failure(reason: concat(
                "MORE THAN ONE TOKEN SHARES THE OBJECT-READ NAME, NOTHING REVOKED: ",
                request.name))
            } else {
              let token_id = fold(
                tokens,
                init: "",
                f: (acc, row) => if row.name == request.name { row.id as String } else { acc },
              )
              if origin_read_pin_is_exact(origin: fabric_durable_origin_standing()) {
                exit_failure(reason: concat(
                  "ORIGIN READ CREDENTIAL IS PINNED TO AN EXACT VERSION, NOTHING REVOKED: the named object-read token is the held mint ",
                  token_id))
              } else {
                let deleted = cloudflare.AccountTokens.Delete(
                  auth_token: bootstrap,
                  account_id: account_id as String,
                  token_id: token_id,
                )
                match deleted.outcome {
                  RestStatusRefused { status: status, body: body } =>
                    if mutation_status_is_commit_ambiguous(status: status) {
                      exit_failure(reason: concat(
                        "REVOKE OUTCOME UNKNOWN, AND THIS RUN WILL NOT DELETE AGAIN: ",
                        body))
                    } else {
                      exit_failure(reason: concat("REVOKE REFUSED: ", body))
                    }
                  RestTransportRefused { cause: cause } =>
                    exit_failure(reason: concat(
                      "REVOKE GOT NO RESPONSE, AND THIS RUN WILL NOT DELETE AGAIN: ",
                      cause as String))
                  RestBodyUndecodable { status: _, cause: cause } =>
                    exit_failure(reason: concat(
                      "REVOKE BODY DID NOT DECODE, AND THIS RUN WILL NOT DELETE AGAIN: ",
                      cause as String))
                  RestOk =>
                    if deleted.success == false {
                      exit_failure(reason: concat(
                        "REVOKE REPORTED HTTP SUCCESS WITH success=false, SO THE TOKEN MAY STILL EXIST: ",
                        token_id))
                    } else {
                      let written = Filesystem.Write(
                        path: "/tmp/r2-object-read-revoke-receipt.txt",
                        content: join(["revoked_token_id=", token_id, "name=", request.name], "\n"),
                      )
                      if written.success {
                        ExitSuccess
                      } else {
                        exit_failure(reason: concat(
                          "REVOKE SUCCEEDED AND THE RECEIPT FILE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN: ",
                          written.error))
                      }
                    }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE ENTRY POINTS. Each reads its subject entirely from declarations -- which account, which
// bucket, which custody locus, which grant -- and takes from the environment only the GCP access
// token path the existing bracket already defines. Nothing about which account or which bucket is
// an environment fact, because a run that could be pointed at a different account by a variable
// would make the origin declaration advisory. The profile is likewise not an argument: `run` is
// the object-read mint that gunbc#10947 executed and `run_object_write` is the object-write mint,
// two entries over one fold, so an operator invoking either knows from the name which credential
// the Create will spend.
//
// A MINT WHOSE CREDENTIAL IS ALREADY PINNED REFUSES BEFORE THE CREATE. Both mints have executed
// and gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin pins both credentials; running either entry again would mint a
// second token the origin declaration does not name and whose value would land as a second version
// nothing reads. This is the origin_read_pin
// check revoke_unheld_object_read_token already performs, applied at the mint rather than only at
// the revoke.
fn run() -> ProcessExit uses net: Network {
  mint_origin_token_and_store(profile: R2OriginObjectRead)
}

fn run_object_write() -> ProcessExit uses net: Network {
  mint_origin_token_and_store(profile: R2OriginObjectWrite)
}

fn origin_credential_already_pinned(profile: R2OriginTokenProfile, origin: R2OriginStanding) -> Bool {
  match origin {
    R2OriginUnallocated { purpose: _ } => false
    R2OriginAllocated { bucket: bucket } =>
      origin_credential_pin_is_exact(credential: r2_origin_token_credential(profile: profile, bucket: bucket))
  }
}

// ── THE ORPHAN RECEIPT, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST AND WAS THE ONE NOT WRITTEN ────────
// A MINT THAT CREATED A TOKEN AND THEN FAILED TO STORE IT IS THE RUN WHOSE RECEIPT DECIDES WHETHER
// THE DAMAGE IS RECOVERABLE. cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create returns a token value exactly once, so
// a store refusal after it leaves a live credential on the account -- and the remedy the refusal
// text itself demands, "the token must be revoked", is only actionable against a token ID. Until
// this, the id was in scope at that moment and thrown away: the success path wrote token_id and the
// refusal path wrote nothing, so the receipt existed for every run that did not need it.
//
// THE ID GOES IN THE EXIT REASON AS WELL AS THE FILE, AND THAT IS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES. The receipt
// is a file write, which is itself a thing that can fail -- and the arm where it would fail is the
// arm where the filesystem is already misbehaving. A remediation that is only recoverable from an
// artifact that may not exist is not located (§5), so the id is put where nothing can swallow it:
// the process's own typed failure. The file is the convenience; the diagnostic is the guarantee.
//
// BOTH ARMS WRITE TO THE SAME PATH BECAUSE THE UPLOADER GLOBS ONE PATH. gunbc.fleet_converge_workflow
// uploads /tmp/r2-*-object-write-mint-receipt.txt with always(), and an orphan receipt written
// somewhere else would be invisible to exactly the run it was written for.
// THE FILENAME CONVENTION IS ONE FACT AND THE UPLOADER READS IT RATHER THAN RE-SPELLING IT.
// review 66010 caught the half of this I had missed: the emitted glob avoided re-spelling the
// BUCKET name, which my annotation defended, while hand-spelling the "/tmp/r2-" prefix and the
// "-mint-receipt.txt" suffix -- exactly the parts this module owns. That drift would be SILENT by
// construction: the upload step is if-no-files-found: warn, so renaming the path here would lose
// the orphan receipt in precisely the run where a live unheld Cloudflare credential exists and its
// id is the only thing that makes the damage recoverable.
// ── THE RECEIPT IS SCOPED TO ONE ATTEMPT, BECAUSE /tmp OUTLIVES THE ATTEMPT ───────────────────
// A SELF-HOSTED RUNNER'S /tmp SURVIVES ACROSS RUNS AND ACROSS ATTEMPTS OF THE SAME RUN, so a shared
// path plus an always() uploader is a route by which a PREVIOUS attempt's receipt is collected as
// THIS attempt's evidence. That is the fabricated-evidence failure aimed at the one artifact whose
// whole purpose is to be trusted after a disaster: an operator reading an orphan receipt is deciding
// which live Cloudflare token to revoke, and a stale receipt names the wrong one.
//
// It is also the shape this PR filed a failure-mode row about, arriving from the other side. There
// the check was correctly written and positioned after the irreversible effect; here the artifact is
// correctly written and can be read as belonging to a run that did not produce it. Both are a
// receipt asserting something the execution does not establish.
//
// SO THE DIRECTORY CARRIES THE ATTEMPT IDENTITY AND THE UPLOADER NAMES ONLY THAT DIRECTORY. A stale
// receipt from attempt N lives under a path attempt N+1's glob cannot match -- structurally, not by
// a cleanup step that must remember to run. The scope SHAPE is declared once here; the two callers
// differ only in where the identity comes from, which is a real difference: the mint reads the
// runner's environment at execution time, the emitter writes a workflow expression evaluated at
// dispatch time. Neither spells the directory.
data r2_mint_receipt_dir_root: String = "/tmp/r2-mint-receipts/"

data r2_mint_receipt_path_prefix: String = "r2-"

data r2_mint_receipt_path_suffix: String = "-mint-receipt.txt"

data r2_mint_receipt_run_id_env: NonEmptyStr = "GITHUB_RUN_ID" as NonEmptyStr

data r2_mint_receipt_run_attempt_env: NonEmptyStr = "GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT" as NonEmptyStr

// PURE, so the separation property is witnessable without a runner. The join is trivial and that is
// the point: both callers must produce the SAME scope for the same attempt, and a different scope
// for a different attempt, and neither may re-spell the separator.
fn r2_mint_receipt_scope(run_id: String, run_attempt: String) -> String {
  join([run_id, "-", run_attempt], "")
}

fn r2_mint_receipt_dir_for(scope: String) -> String {
  join([r2_mint_receipt_dir_root, scope, "/"], "")
}

// AN UNSCOPED RUN IS A SESSION RUN, AND IT IS NOT THE HAZARD. Off a runner there is no run id, no
// second attempt, and no uploader; the receipt is read by the operator who just ran the entry. The
// arm is named rather than defaulted so that a WORKFLOW run missing the identity cannot quietly
// reuse it -- the uploader names an attempt directory, so an unscoped receipt is collected by
// nothing rather than collected as the wrong attempt's.
type R2MintReceiptScope
  = R2MintReceiptAttemptScoped { scope: String }
  | R2MintReceiptUnscoped

fn r2_mint_receipt_dir(scope: R2MintReceiptScope) -> String {
  match scope {
    R2MintReceiptAttemptScoped { scope: s } => r2_mint_receipt_dir_for(scope: s)
    R2MintReceiptUnscoped => r2_mint_receipt_dir_root
  }
}

fn r2_mint_receipt_path_in(scope: R2MintReceiptScope, request: CreateAccountTokenRequest) -> String {
  join([
    r2_mint_receipt_dir(scope: scope),
    r2_mint_receipt_path_prefix,
    request.name,
    r2_mint_receipt_path_suffix,
  ], "")
}

// THE GLOB THE EMITTED UPLOADER USES, DERIVED FROM THE SAME PARTS AND NOW SCOPED. The wildcard still
// stands for the bucket name, which is not knowable at emit time; the directory is not a wildcard,
// which is what makes a previous attempt's receipt unmatchable rather than merely unlikely.
fn r2_mint_receipt_glob_in(scope: R2MintReceiptScope, profile: R2OriginTokenProfile) -> String {
  join([
    r2_mint_receipt_dir(scope: scope),
    r2_mint_receipt_path_prefix,
    "*",
    r2_origin_token_name_suffix(profile: profile),
    r2_mint_receipt_path_suffix,
  ], "")
}

// THE SCOPE THIS RUN IS IN, READ FROM THE RUNNER'S OWN ENVIRONMENT. Both variables are set by GitHub
// on every runner; requiring BOTH means a half-populated environment scopes nothing rather than
// scoping to a partial identity that a later attempt could collide with.
// THE RECEIPT DIRECTORY IS A PRECONDITION OF THE MINT, NOT A STEP BESIDE THE WRITE, and the reason
// is this PR's own thesis applied to the repair it just made. Filesystem.Write is realized as bare
// std::fs::write, which does NOT create parents, so scoping the receipt under
// /tmp/r2-mint-receipts/<run>-<attempt>/ made the write fail with ENOENT on every workflow run --
// both GITHUB_RUN_ID and GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT are always set there, so every mint took the nested
// path (review 66170).
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE WAS THE WORST ONE AVAILABLE, which is why this is established BEFORE the Create
// rather than checked beside the write. After a SUCCESSFUL store the failed receipt turned
// ExitSuccess into a failure; the origin pin is a declaration edit, so origin_credential_already_pinned
// stays false, and a re-dispatch would spend ANOTHER non-idempotent AccountTokens.Create. A safety
// repair that manufactures a double-mint path is worse than the hazard it closed.
//
// Ensuring it ahead of the Create means a filesystem that cannot hold the receipt refuses with
// NOTHING SPENT -- the same ordering the custody container now has, for the same reason.
type R2MintReceiptDirStanding
  = R2MintReceiptDirReady
  | R2MintReceiptDirRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn ensure_r2_mint_receipt_dir(scope: R2MintReceiptScope) -> R2MintReceiptDirStanding {
  let dir = r2_mint_receipt_dir(scope: scope)
  let made = shell.Mkdir.Parents(path: dir as FilePath)
  if made.success {
    R2MintReceiptDirReady
  } else {
    R2MintReceiptDirRefused {
      cause: join([
        "THE RECEIPT DIRECTORY COULD NOT BE CREATED AT ", dir,
        ", so a mint would have nowhere to record the token id it may need revoked (mkdir -p refused; extdeps.shell Mkdir.Parents reports only exit status, so there is no errno to carry here)",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  }
}

fn r2_mint_receipt_scope_for_this_run() -> R2MintReceiptScope {
  match shell.Env.Get(name: r2_mint_receipt_run_id_env).value {
    Absent => R2MintReceiptUnscoped
    Present { value: run_id } =>
      match shell.Env.Get(name: r2_mint_receipt_run_attempt_env).value {
        Absent => R2MintReceiptUnscoped
        Present { value: run_attempt } =>
          if trim(s: run_id) == "" || trim(s: run_attempt) == "" {
            R2MintReceiptUnscoped
          } else {
            R2MintReceiptAttemptScoped {
              scope: r2_mint_receipt_scope(run_id: trim(s: run_id), run_attempt: trim(s: run_attempt)),
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn r2_orphan_receipt_content(
  token: CreatedAccountToken,
  cause: NonEmptyStr,
  custody: R2MintedCustodyStanding,
) -> String {
  join(
    [
      concat("token_id=", token.id as String),
      minted_custody_line(custody: custody),
      concat("store_refusal=", cause as String),
      minted_custody_action(custody: custody),
      "reconcile=gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/cloudflare/r2_token_mint_run.dag --function list_account_tokens_for_reconcile",
    ],
    "\n",
  )
}

// THE FAILURE IS RETURNED WHETHER OR NOT THE RECEIPT LANDED, and the two cases are distinguishable
// in the text. A receipt that could not be written must not upgrade the outcome, and it must not be
// silently identical to one that was written either -- an operator reading the diagnostic has to
// know whether there is a file to go and look at.
fn refuse_with_orphan_receipt(
  token: CreatedAccountToken,
  request: CreateAccountTokenRequest,
  cause: NonEmptyStr,
  custody: R2MintedCustodyStanding,
) -> ProcessExit {
  let path = r2_mint_receipt_path_in(scope: r2_mint_receipt_scope_for_this_run(), request: request)
  let written = Filesystem.Write(
    path: path,
    content: r2_orphan_receipt_content(token: token, cause: cause, custody: custody),
  )
  if written.success {
    exit_failure(reason: join([
      "STORE REFUSED AFTER THE TOKEN WAS MINTED, SO A LIVE CREDENTIAL EXISTS -- token_id=",
      token.id as String, " ", minted_custody_line(custody: custody), ": ", cause as String,
      " || ", minted_custody_action(custody: custody), " || receipt written to ", path,
    ], ""))
  } else {
    exit_failure(reason: join([
      "STORE REFUSED AFTER THE TOKEN WAS MINTED, SO A LIVE CREDENTIAL EXISTS -- token_id=",
      token.id as String, " ", minted_custody_line(custody: custody), ": ", cause as String,
      " || ", minted_custody_action(custody: custody),
      " || THE RECEIPT FILE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN EITHER, so this diagnostic is the only record of that id: ",
      written.error,
    ], ""))
  }
}

fn mint_origin_token_and_store(profile: R2OriginTokenProfile) -> ProcessExit uses net: Network {
  if origin_credential_already_pinned(profile: profile, origin: fabric_durable_origin_standing()) {
    exit_failure(reason: concat(
      "THE ORIGIN ALREADY PINS THIS CREDENTIAL TO AN EXACT VERSION, NOTHING CREATED: a second mint would leave a token on the account that no declaration names; rotate by editing the pin in gunbc.cloudflare.r2_origin first -- ",
      r2_origin_token_secret_id(profile: profile) as String))
  } else {
  match r2_mint_bootstrap(profile: profile, requirement: ObserveCustodyContainer) {
    R2MintBootstrapRefused { cause: cause } =>
      exit_failure(reason: concat(concat(
        cause as String, ", NOTHING CREATED || the dashboard ceremony this is waiting on: "),
        bootstrap_burden_text()))
    R2MintBootstrapReady {
      account_id: account_id,
      request: request,
      bootstrap: bootstrap,
      gcp_token: gcp_token,
      resolved_version: _,
      container: container,
    } =>
      match container {
        Absent =>
          exit_failure(reason: "THE CUSTODY CONTAINER WAS NOT OBSERVED FOR A RUN THAT WOULD WRITE ONE, NOTHING CREATED: the mint asked for the observation and the bracket returned none, so this run cannot show the destination exists and will not spend a Create to find out")
        Present { value: container } =>
      match ensure_r2_mint_receipt_dir(scope: r2_mint_receipt_scope_for_this_run()) {
        R2MintReceiptDirRefused { cause: dir_cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: concat(dir_cause as String, " -- NOTHING CREATED"))
        R2MintReceiptDirReady =>
      match mint_r2_origin_token(
        account_id: account_id,
        request: request,
        bootstrap: bootstrap,
      ) {
        R2TokenMintRefused { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: concat("MINT REFUSED: ", cause as String))
        R2TokenMintOutcomeUnknown { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: concat("MINT OUTCOME UNKNOWN, AND THIS RUN WILL NOT CREATE AGAIN: ", cause as String))
        R2TokenMinted { token: token, pair: _ } =>
          match store_minted_token(
            token: token,
            locus: fleet_secret_ref(secret_id: r2_origin_token_secret_id(profile: profile)),
            gcp_token: gcp_token,
            container: container,
          ) {
            R2CredentialStoreRefused { cause: cause, custody: custody } =>
              refuse_with_orphan_receipt(token: token, request: request, cause: cause, custody: custody)
            R2CredentialStoredAt { version_resource: version_resource } => {
              let written = Filesystem.Write(
                path: r2_mint_receipt_path_in(scope: r2_mint_receipt_scope_for_this_run(), request: request),
                content: join(
                  [
                    concat("token_id=", token.id as String),
                    concat("version_resource=", version_resource),
                  ],
                  "\n",
                ),
              )
              if written.success {
                ExitSuccess
              } else {
                exit_failure(reason: concat(
                  "TOKEN WAS MINTED AND STORED BUT THE NON-SECRET RECEIPT FILE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN: ",
                  written.error))
              }
            }
          }
      }
      }
      }
  }
  }
}

// THE REFUSED ADMISSION IS RENDERED BY ARM, because the three arms name three different authoring
// defects -- a shape whose acquisition says the dashboard is the only route, a policy whose
// permission groups disagree with the shape's grants, and a lifetime the request contradicts -- and
// one message covering all three would send the reader to the wrong declaration.
fn mint_refusal_text(standing: AccountTokenApiMintStanding) -> String {
  match standing {
    AccountTokenApiMintAdmitted { shape: _, request: _ } =>
      "the mint was admitted, which is not a refusal; reaching this arm means the classifier routed an admitted standing into the refused position"
    AccountTokenApiMintRefusedHumanDashboardOnly { shape: _ } =>
      "the token shape says HumanDashboardAcquired, so AccountTokens.Create is not its route; only a shape acquired CreatedViaAccountTokensApi may be minted"
    AccountTokenApiMintRefusedGrantPolicyMismatch { shape: _, request: _ } =>
      "the request's policy permission groups do not agree with the shape's grants, so the token created would not carry the access the shape claims"
    AccountTokenApiMintRefusedLifetimeMismatch { shape: _, request: _ } =>
      "the request's expiry does not agree with the shape's declared lifetime"
  }
}
